  1130                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1131                              <1> ;  Console layer
  1132                              <1> ;
  1133                              <1> ;  putc prints AL. waitk is the host's key routine: it waits up to EAX ms
  1134                              <1> ;  (0: just a look; -1: for ever) for a key, and returns it in AL with the
  1135                              <1> ;  zero flag clear, or AL = 0 with the zero flag set if none came. Both keep
  1136                              <1> ;  every register but EAX (putc keeps EAX too).
  1137                              <1> ;
  1138                              <1> ;  A running program reads the keyboard only through getk and pollk, which
  1139                              <1> ;  between them give the break key its rules:
  1140                              <1> ;    - Ctrl-C stops the program;
  1141                              <1> ;    - any other key that the break check reads is held at HELD for the
  1142                              <1> ;      next '?', and a newer one takes its place;
  1143                              <1> ;    - '?' (getk) takes the held key first;
  1144                              <1> ;    - a program that has stored a non-zero byte at BRKOFF gets Ctrl-C as a
  1145                              <1> ;      key like any other, but two of them within about 250 ms still stop it.
  1146                              <1> ;  Both are called straight from a handler (or from rpoll), never from a
  1147                              <1> ;  routine under one: the checked build's report counts on finding one
  1148                              <1> ;  return address above the data stack.
  1149                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1150                              <1> 
  1151                              <1> ; putc: WriteFile to the output handle (standard output; the checked
  1152                              <1> ; report switches it to standard error), one byte. PUSHAD saves everything;
  1153                              <1> ; the byte is AL in the saved EAX, and the count goes in the slot of the
  1154                              <1> ; saved ESP, which POPAD ignores.
  1155 000004D0 60                  <1> putc:   pushad
  1156 000004D1 8D4C240C            <1>         lea     ecx,[esp+12]
  1157 000004D5 8D54241C            <1>         lea     edx,[esp+28]
  1158 000004D9 6A00                <1>         push    0                       ; lpOverlapped
  1159 000004DB 51                  <1>         push    ecx                     ; lpNumberOfBytesWritten
  1160 000004DC 6A01                <1>         push    1
  1161 000004DE 52                  <1>         push    edx
  1162 000004DF FF7552              <1>         push    dword [V(v_outh)]
  1163 000004E2 FF55B1              <1>         call    [V(iat_WriteFile)]
  1164 000004E5 61                  <1>         popad
  1165 000004E6 C3                  <1>         ret
  1166                              <1> 
  1167                              <1> ; waitk. At a console: a wait on the input handle for the time (EAX: 0
  1168                              <1> ; just looks, -1 is INFINITE), then ReadConsoleInputA for one event. The
  1169                              <1> ; wait is WaitForMultipleObjects, on v_hin alone or on v_hin and v_htm,
  1170                              <1> ; which follow each other. A timed wait (1 ms or more) sets the
  1171                              <1> ; high-resolution timer once for the whole time and waits for the input or
  1172                              <1> ; the timer, with no time of its own; without the timer it is a plain wait
  1173                              <1> ; on the input for the time, which starts again with its whole time after
  1174                              <1> ; each event passed over. Only key-down events count: their character,
  1175                              <1> ; or for the left and right arrow keys (virtual keys 25h and 27h, which
  1176                              <1> ; have none) 1Ch and 1Eh, which the editor takes for them (a typed ^B or ^F
  1177                              <1> ; does nothing there). Anything else is passed over.
  1178                              <1> ; Input that is not a console (a pipe or a file) is read a byte at a time
  1179                              <1> ; with ReadFile, and only by a wait that is not just a look (a look would
  1180                              <1> ; eat the input); at its end the interpreter exits.
